> get items or create and kill monsters to gain levels?

I don't think there's any one behavior that all successful
players must engage in (unless you count being careful and
learning a lot about the game as behaviors; I'm pretty sure
those are both required).  There are many different ways to
win at NetHack.  In fact, I think most really successful
players have to learn multiple different techniques and
vary them depending on the circumstances in the game.  (One
of the most important circumstances is role.  If you're a
chaotic elvish wizard, you're not going to play the same
way as a lawful dwarvish valkyrie.)

Using pets to steal from shops is one behavior that a lot
of advanced players do use, but be careful:  spending too
much time messing around with shops (especially in the
early game) can lead to running out of food.  Also, players
who spend a lot of time stealing from shops are usually
less interested in the items than in the shopkeeper's gold,
which they use to buy protection from a priest, usually in
Minetown.  Some players prefer to collect all the junk
that's laying around in the dungeon (scrap armor,
particularly) and sell it.  Depending on role and/or
the availability of an early touchstone, you may be able to
use gems instead.  It doesn't take very many valuable gems
to clear out a shop of all the gold and items.

Other useful behaviors that it's good to know how to do:
 * Use your pet to curse-test armor and weapons.  Test
   all the non-cursed armor by wearing it, and keep
   the best armor and weapons you find.
 * Use resources efficiently, when possible.  Sometimes
   you should "waste" an item (use it inefficiently)
   because doing so will give you an immediate survival
   advantage; but frequently it's better to save things
   until you can use them well.  For example, scrolls
   of identify are best used when they are blessed, and
   you ideally want to be holding as many unidentified
   items as possible.
 * Train your pet, let it kill things, and protect it when
   possible, but don't hesitate to put it in harm's way to
   save yourself when necessary.
 * Learn to save when you are frustrated or impatient
   and come back later.
 * Whenever possible, figure out what items are without
   formally identifying them.  It saves scrolls.
 * Manage your skills effectively.  Train the ones you're
   going to need, advancing them when it makes sense.
 * Kill dangerous enemies at a distance.  Use a wide
   variety of tricks to keep them from touching you.
 * Engrave Elbereth frequently.  Pray infrequently,
   or sacrifice to reduce your prayer timeout to zero.
 * Use telepathy and a blindfold to preview what
   monsters are on each new level.
 * Eat things that may grant useful intrinsics.
   (If you don't have a lot of food, eat any corpse
   that's safe to eat.)
 * Do things that exercise your attributes.
 * Maximize your luck.
 * Obsessively check your armor to make sure you are still
   wearing what you thought you were wearing.  That goes
   double for gloves and triple for anything that grants
   reflection, levitation, or magic resistance.
 * Don't fight while burdened.  Don't flee while burdened.
   Don't explore while burdened.
 * Stash useful or dangerous objects where monsters can't
   easily get them.
 * Unless you are a Tourist, allocate your scrolls of
   charging to the most important things.  Usually this
   means wands of wishing first, followed by magic markers,
   followed by wands of death.
 * Get as much divine protection as you can.  It's worth
   digging out vaults just for this.
 * Enchant your armor as high as you can without
   vaporizing it.  Weapons also.
 * When you need a number of things but have a lot of
   stuff you don't need, polypile.
 * Use cursing and confusion to change what scrolls do.
 * Take advantage of polymorph traps to get powerful
   pets.
 * Complete Sokoban as soon as you can safely do so.
 * Get to Mine's End as soon as you can safely do so.
 * Before every single keypress, stop and ask yourself,
   "Is there some way this could kill me?"


